  "worked_solution": "Using the transformed equation, we solve for $Y(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}Y(s)+5sY(s)+4Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+5s+4)Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s} \\\\ (s+1)(s+4)Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s} \\\\ Y(s) & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s(s+1)(s+4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the inverse transform, we first use partial fraction decomposition on the algebraic part:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{s(s+1)(s+4)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{B}{s+1}+\\frac{C}{s+4}\n$$\n\nSolving for the constants, we find $A=\\frac{1}{4}$, $B=-\\frac{1}{3}$, and $C=\\frac{1}{12}$, giving:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{s(s+1)(s+4)}=\\frac{1}{4s}-\\frac{1}{3(s+1)}+\\frac{1}{12(s+4)}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we apply the inverse Laplace transform. Let \n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{4s}-\\frac{1}{3(s+1)}+\\frac{1}{12(s+4)},\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nf(t)=\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-t}+\\frac{1}{12}{e}^{-4t}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the second shifting theorem \n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}{{e}^{-as}F(s)}={u}_{a}(t)f(t-a).\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={L}^{-1}{{e}^{-3s}F(s)} \\\\ y(t) & ={u}_{3}(t)\\,f(t-3) \\\\ y(t) & =u(t-3)(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-(t-3)}+\\frac{1}{12}{e}^{-4(t-3)}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
